                "content": "**Protein context**\n\n* Mature protein begins at residue\u202f26 (the signal peptide 1\u201125 is removed).  \n* Hydrophobic stretches: **H1**\u202f=\u202f180\u2011202, **H2**\u202f=\u202f300\u2011322.  \n* Potential N\u2011linked sequons: **S90**, **S250**, **S350** (centered on residues\u202f90,\u202f250,\u202f350).  \n\nThe experimental observations are summarised below  \n\n| Observation | Interpretation |\n|-------------|----------------|\n| S90 and S350 are glycosylated; S250 is not | Only residues that are exposed to the ER lumen while the nascent chain is being translocated receive glycans. |\n| Protease (in intact microsomes) digests almost everything between H1 and H2, but leaves fragments that contain residues\u202f90 and\u202f350 untouched | The protected fragments are lumenal (protease cannot reach the lumen). The digested region is cytosolic. |\n| Detergent\u202f+\u202fprotease gives complete digestion | When the membrane barrier is removed, protease can attack any exposed polypeptide. |\n| In the \u0394H2 construct, S90 is still glycosylated, S250 and S350 are not, and *all* residues C\u2011terminal to H1 are protease\u2011sensitive | Without H2 the C\u2011terminal domain exits the membrane into the cytosol. |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Membrane topology of the WT protein (post\u2011signal\u2011peptide cleavage)\n\n| Segment | Position (aa) | Topology inferred from the data |\n|---------|---------------|----------------------------------|\n| **Residues\u202f26\u2011179** | 26\u2013179 | **Lumenal** \u2013 contains S90, which is glycosylated \u2192 exposed to lumen. |\n| **H1** | 180\u2011202 | **Transmembrane** \u2013 first hydrophobic stretch that inserts into the membrane. |\n| **Residues\u202f203\u2011299** | 203\u2013299 | **Cytosolic** \u2013 protease removes this region in intact microsomes; S250 (within this region) is not glycosylated. |\n| **H2** | 300\u2011322 | **Transmembrane** \u2013 second hydrophobic stretch that re\u2011enters the membrane. |\n| **Residues\u202f323\u2011420** | 323\u2013420 | **Lumenal** \u2013 contains S350, which is glycosylated; the whole C\u2011terminal tail is protected from protease. |\n\nThus the overall topology is:\n\n```\nN\u2011terminus (26) \u2500\u2500\u25ba lumen \u2500\u2500\u25ba H1 (TM1) \u2500\u2500\u25ba cytosol \u2500\u2500\u25ba H2 (TM2) \u2500\u2500\u25ba lumen \u25c4\u2500\u2500 C\u2011terminus (420)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Which internal hydrophobic segment is a **stop\u2011transfer** and which is a **start\u2011transfer**?\n\n* **H1 (180\u2011202)** \u2013 **Start\u2011transfer**  \n  *It is the first hydrophobic segment encountered after the signal peptide. Its insertion halts the continuous emergence of the nascent chain into the lumen and redirects the growing polypeptide into the membrane, producing a cytosolic loop.*\n\n* **H2 (300\u2011322)** \u2013 **Stop\u2011transfer**  \n  *After H1 the chain traverses the membrane and emerges into the cytosol. H2 inserts again and **stops** further translocation; the remainder of the chain (residues\u202f323\u2011420) stays on the lumenal side of the membrane.*  \n\n**Evidence from glycosylation**\n\n* **S90** (luminal, before H1) is glycosylated \u2192 the chain is in the lumen prior to H1.  \n* **S250** (between H1 and H2) is **not** glycosylated \u2192 the segment is cytosolic, confirming that H1 has already brought the chain into the cytosol.  \n* **S350** (after H2) is glycosylated \u2192 the C\u2011terminal domain remains lumenal, showing that H2 functions as a stop\u2011transfer element that prevents the chain from re\u2011entering the cytosol.\n\nIf H2 were a start\u2011transfer segment, the C\u2011terminal tail would have been cytosolic and S350 would not have been glycosylated, contrary to observation.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Why is the sequon at residue\u202f250 not glycosylated?\n\nThe sequon at 250 lies in the **cytosolic loop** between H1 and H2. Glycosylation by the ER oligosaccharyltransferase (OST) can occur only when the sequon is **exposed to the ER lumen** while the nascent chain is being translocated. Once the chain has passed through H1, it is on the cytosolic face of the membrane; the OST cannot act on residues that are now cytosolic. Consequently, S250 never experiences lumen exposure and therefore remains unglycosylated.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Predictions for the \u0394H2 construct (H2 deleted)\n\n*Without H2 the chain has no stop\u2011transfer element after H1.*  \n\n1. **Location of the C\u2011terminus (residues\u202f323\u2011420):** Cytosolic. After traversing H1 the nascent chain continues through the membrane and exits into the cytosol; there is nothing to retain it in the lumen.  \n\n2. **Glycosylation state of residue\u202f350:** **Not glycosylated.** Because the C\u2011terminal tail is cytosolic, the sequon at 350 is never presented to the lumenal OST.  \n\n3. **Agreement with protease\u2011protection data:** In the \u0394H2 construct \u201cessentially all residues C\u2011terminal to H1 are protease\u2011sensitive\u201d in intact microsomes. This exactly matches the prediction that the entire C\u2011terminal region is cytosolic and therefore accessible to the externally added protease. The loss of glycosylation at 350 further confirms that the region is no longer lumenal.\n\n---\n\n## 5. How many membrane\u2011spanning segments does the mature WT protein possess?\n\nThe mature protein (starting at residue\u202f26) contains **two** internal hydrophobic stretches that traverse the membrane:\n\n* **H1 (180\u2011202)** \u2013 first transmembrane segment.  \n* **H2 (300\u2011322)** \u2013 second transmembrane segment.\n\nThe original signal peptide (residues\u202f1\u201125) is **not** counted because it is removed by signal\u2011peptidase after its function as a targeting/anchoring sequence is fulfilled. The remaining N\u2011terminus (residue\u202f26 onward) is already in the lumen and is not embedded in the membrane. Hence the mature WT protein has **2 membrane\u2011spanning segments**.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to answer multiple subquestions about membrane topology.\n\nGiven data:\n\nWT protein (420 aa). Signal peptide 1-25 cleaved. So mature protein starts at residue 26.\n\nHydrophobic segments: H1 residues 180-202 (23 aa). H2 residues 300-322 (23 aa).\n\nPotential N-linked glycosylation sequons centered at residues 90, 250, 350.\n\nObservations:\n\n- After signal peptide cleavage, sequons at 90 and 350 are glycosylated; sequon at 250 is not glycosylated.\n\n- Protease treatment of intact microsomes removes most of segment between H1 and H2, but protected fragments containing residue 90 and residue 350 remain. Means those residues are protected from protease => they are inside lumen (or within membrane?). Since protease cannot access lumen side, protection indicates they are either in lumen or within membrane (transmembrane) where protease can't reach. But we know glycosylation occurs only if sequon exposed to lumen during translocation. So residues 90 and 350 are glycosylated -> they were exposed to lumen. Therefore they are lumenal (i.e., extracellular side of ER). The protected fragments contain them, meaning they are not accessible to protease because they're inside lumen.\n\n- Treatment with detergent + protease leads to complete digestion, indicating that without membrane barrier, protease can digest everything.\n\nSecond construct lacking H2:\n\n- Residue 90 still glycosylated.\n\n- Residues 250 and 350 not glycosylated.\n\n- Essentially all residues C-terminal to H1 are protease-sensitive in intact microsomes. That means after removal of H2, the region after H1 becomes cytosolic (accessible to protease), except maybe some parts? It says essentially all residues C-terminal to H1 are protease-sensitive. So the C-terminus ends up cytosolic.\n\nThus H2 likely functions as a stop-transfer sequence that retains the C-terminal domain in lumen. Without H2, the protein continues through the membrane, exiting into cytosol.\n\nNow we need to infer topology.\n\nLet's think: Starting from N-terminus after signal peptide cleavage (residue 26). We have region 26-179 before H1. Then H1 (180-202) is a hydrophobic segment. After H1, region 203-299. Then H2 (300-322). Then C-terminal region 323-420.\n\nWe need to assign each region as lumenal, transmembrane, or cytosolic.\n\nFrom data: Residue 90 is glycosylated, so it must be lumenal. That's within region 26-179 (since 90 < 180). So region 26-179 is lumenal (extracellular side). Good.\n\nResidue 350 is glycosylated in WT, but note that 350 lies after H2 (since H2 ends at 322). So region 323-420 includes residue 350. Yet it's glycosylated, implying that part of the C-terminal tail is lumenal despite being after H2. This suggests that H2 is a stop-transfer sequence that keeps the C-terminal domain in lumen. So region 323-420 is lumenal.\n\nResidue 250 is not glycosylated. Where is 250? It's between H1 and H2 (since H1 ends at 202, H2 starts at 300). So region 203-299 includes 250. Not glycosylated suggests that this region is not exposed to lumen; perhaps it's cytosolic (inside the cell) or within membrane? However protease protection shows that segment between H1 and H2 is mostly removed by protease, meaning it's cytosolic (protease can access). Protected fragments contain residues 90 and 350, not 250. So indeed region 203-299 is cytosolic.\n\nThus topology: N-terminus (after signal peptide) emerges into lumen, then goes back across membrane via H1 (first TM segment), then cytosolic loop (region 203-299), then second TM segment H2, then lumenal C-terminal tail (323-420). So H1 is a transmembrane segment (TM1). H2 is also a transmembrane segment (TM2). The region 26-179 is lumenal (extracellular). Region 203-299 is cytosolic. Region 323-420 is lumenal.\n\nNow answer Q1 accordingly.\n\nQ2: Identify which internal hydrophobic segment acts as stop-transfer vs start-transfer. Typically start-transfer is first TM that stops further translocation? Actually start-transfer is the first TM that initiates insertion into membrane, causing the nascent chain to become anchored; subsequent segments may be stop-transfer (prevent further passage) or start-transfer again (allow continuation). In our case, H1 appears to be start-transfer (first TM) that brings the N-terminal lumenal domain across membrane into cytosol, then H2 is stop-transfer that prevents the C-terminal domain from entering cytosol, keeping it lumenal. Evidence: Glycosylation of residues 90 (luminal) and 350 (C-terminal) indicates that after H2, the chain stays lumenal. If H2 were start-transfer, the C-terminal would go cytosolic, not glycosylated. Also protease sensitivity: region between H1 and H2 is cytosolic (digestible); region after H2 is protected (luminal). Thus H2 is stop-transfer.\n\nExplain using glycosylation: Because sequon at 350 is glycosylated, the C-terminal domain must be exposed to lumen, consistent with H2 acting as stop-transfer. Conversely, sequon at 250 not glycosylated indicates that region is not lumenal, consistent with H2 preventing its exposure.\n\nQ3: Why sequon at 250 not glycosylated? Because it resides in the cytosolic loop between H1 and H2, which is not exposed to lumen; glycosylation requires lumen exposure. Additionally, the nascent chain passes through H1 into cytosol, then H2 blocks further entry, so the segment between them never reaches lumen.\n\nAlso could mention that the enzyme oligosaccharyltransferase (OST) adds glycans co-translationally as the nascent chain emerges into lumen; once the chain has passed through H1 into cytosol, the OST cannot act on residues that are now cytosolic.\n\nQ4: Predict location of C terminus and glycosylation state of residue 350 when H2 is deleted. Without H2, there is no stop-transfer; after H1, the chain continues through membrane and exits into cytosol. Therefore C terminus (residues 323-420) will be cytosolic. Consequently, sequon at 350 will not be glycosylated because it will not be exposed to lumen. This matches observed protease-protection: all residues C-terminal to H1 become protease-sensitive (cytosolic). So prediction: C terminus cytosolic, residue 350 not glycosylated.\n\nExplain agreement: In the \u0394H2 construct, protease can access the entire C-terminal region, confirming cytosolic location; lack of glycosylation confirms absence of lumen exposure.\n\nQ5: Number of membrane-spanning segments in mature WT protein after signal peptide cleavage. According to topology, there are two internal hydrophobic segments (H1 and H2) that span membrane. The signal peptide was cleaved off, so it does not count as a spanning segment of the mature protein. Even though the signal peptide initially inserted into membrane, after cleavage the remaining N-terminus is lumenal and not embedded. Therefore total = 2.\n\nExplain why not count signal peptide: because after cleavage, the N-terminal portion (residues 1-25) is removed; the mature protein's N-terminus begins at residue 26, which is already in lumen; the former signal peptide is no longer part of the protein.\n\nNow craft final answer with clear sections.\n\n"
